{
  "id": "phys.3.em",

changes.after:
        "scenario": "phys.3.energy-work"
      }
    }
  ],
  "learning_outcomes": [
    "Use signed momentum and relative speed to predict one-dimensional collisions of unequal masses.",
    "Distinguish kinetic-energy conservation from total-energy conservation, including changes of inertial reference frame.",
    "Recover impulse from the area under a force–time graph and compare stopping durations at fixed velocity change.",
    "Use the work–energy theorem through sliding, stopping and reversal with gravity and friction.",
    "Separate signed displacement from distance travelled when accounting for gravitational and frictional energy changes.",
    "Convert power and elapsed time to transferred energy while accounting for every output channel."
  ],
  "lesson": {
    "overview": "Conservation laws connect a trajectory to an account of momentum and energy. Momentum is signed in one dimension. Kinetic energy is nonnegative, while potential energy depends on the chosen zero. The workbench calculates positions, velocities, work and energy in SI units under explicit idealized assumptions.",
    "worked_example": "In the sticking preset, a 2 kg cart travelling at 4 m/s catches a stationary 1 kg cart. Total momentum is 8 kg·m/s, so both leave at 8/3 m/s. Initial kinetic energy is 16 J; final kinetic energy is 32/3 J. The difference, 16/3 J, becomes internal energy. In the centre-of-mass frame the total momentum is zero and the final kinetic energy is zero, but the same 16/3 J is transferred internally.",
    "activity": "Use the collision timeline before and after contact, then select the centre-of-mass frame. Compare short and long stopping pulses without changing the initial and final velocities. In the work station choose “Climb, stop and slide back”: inspect the zero-velocity event and compare signed displacement with total travel. Then choose “50 N push: no displacement” and explain why mechanical work is zero. In the power station verify that 60 W for 5 minutes transfers 18,000 J.",
    "reflection": "Which quantities depend on the observer? Why does friction continue increasing internal energy after a mass reverses direction? Why does a longer contact time lower force only when the impulse is held fixed? A zero-work force can still be large: what other energy transfers might occur inside a person pushing a stationary crate? The prescribed force pulse does not predict damage, and the straight-slope model does not replace a pendulum trajectory."
  }
},
